;; Endeavor 152 rung 03 — the EFFECTIVE cluster extent must appear in the metadata.
;;
;; WHY THIS RUNG EXISTS, and it is not bookkeeping.  A kernel whose cluster silently collapsed
;; to extent 1 still computes the correct answer.  Every multicast becomes an ordinary
;; per-workgroup load, the bandwidth win the declaration was written for is gone, and NOTHING in
;; the output reveals it.  That is the 143 failure class exactly — two bugs there let a benchmark
;; report 69.65 TFLOPS for a kernel running at 1.40, and correctness testing could not see it.
;;
;; So the effective extent — what the compiler actually built, not what the source asked for —
;; has to be an assertable artefact.  A benchmark harness or a spec can then check that the
;; cluster formed, instead of inferring it from a timing number that has many other explanations.
;;
;; The distinction matters: `:set-to 4` in the source and an effective extent of 4 are two
;; different facts, and on a target without cluster support the second is 1 while the first is
;; still 4.  The metadata must report the SECOND.
;;
;; NEW VALIDATOR REQUIRED: validate-metacrisp-cluster-extent — assert the kernel's metadata
;; records an effective cluster extent, and that it matches the declaration on a capable target.
;;
;; COMPILE-WITH[--ir-target=ptx --ir-target-arch=sm_90]: PASS
;; TEST-WITH[--metadata --ir-target=ptx --ir-target-arch=sm_90] : validate-metacrisp-cluster-extent
;;
;; UNDER --differentiate THIS ASSERTS THE OPPOSITE, and that is deliberate.  `--metadata
;; --differentiate` emits only the BACKWARD kernel's metacrisp (the forward's comes from an
;; ordinary compile), and a backward kernel SHOULD NOT carry cluster-size at all.  Endeavour
;; 146's thesis: scheduling is not mathematics.  cluster-size says where the bytes arrive, not
;; what is computed, so a derivative has no use for it -- and its presence there would mean a
;; schedule had leaked into the math.  The validator therefore flips: on a forward metacrisp it
;; asserts the cluster formed, on a backward one it asserts nothing leaked.

(def-type mat-t (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

(def-kernel cluster_metadata (A &out C)
  (declare #'(mat-t &out mat-t)
           (global-size :set-to 128)
           (local-size  :set-to 32)
           (cluster-size :set-to 4 :msg "four workgroups per cluster"))
  (let ((tid (to-int (get-global-linear-id))))
    (set! (~ C 0 tid) (~ A 0 tid))))
